      "text": "To solve the problem, we will follow the relationships given step by step.\n\n1. **Identify the given values and relationships:**\n   - The value of \\( y \\) is one-fifth the value of \\( z \\).\n   - The value of \\( x \\) is one-half the value of \\( y \\).\n   - The value of \\( z \\) is 60.\n\n2. **Calculate the value of \\( y \\):**\n   - Since \\( y \\) is one-fifth the value of \\( z \\) and \\( z = 60 \\),\n     \\[\n     y = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 60 = 12.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the value of \\( x \\):**\n   - Since \\( x \\) is one-half the value of \\( y \\) and \\( y = 12 \\),\n     \\[\n     x = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 = 6.\n     \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\nGiven the sets $P=\\{x|-x^2+2x+3\\geq0\\}$ and $Q=\\{x|x>1\\}$, which of the following statements is correct?\nA: $Q \\subseteq P$\nB: $P \\subseteq Q$\nC: $(\\complement_R P)\\subseteq Q$\nD: $P \\subseteq (\\complement_R Q)$\n\nTo determine which statement is correct, we need to first find the sets \\( P \\) and \\( \\complement_R P \\), and then compare them with the sets \\( Q \\) and \\( \\complement_R Q \\).\n\nFirst, let's find the set \\( P \\). The set \\( P \\) is defined as:\n\\[ P = \\{ x \\mid -x^2 + 2x + 3 \\geq 0 \\} \\]\nWe solve the inequality \\( -x^2 + 2x + 3 \\geq 0 \\). This can be rewritten as:\n\\[ x^2 - 2x - 3 \\leq 0 \\]\nWe factor the quadratic expression:\n\\[ (x - 3)(x + 1) \\leq 0 \\]\nThe roots of the equation \\( (x - 3)(x + 1) = 0 \\) are \\( x = -1 \\) and \\( x = 3 \\). The quadratic expression \\( (x - 3)(x + 1) \\) is a parabola that opens upwards, so it is less than or equal to zero between the roots. Therefore, the solution to the inequality is:\n\\[ -1 \\leq x \\leq 3 \\]\nThus, the set \\( P \\) is:\n\\[ P = [-1, 3] \\]\n\nNext, we find the set \\( \\complement_R P \\), which is the complement of \\( P \\) in the real numbers \\( \\mathbb{R} \\):\n\\[ \\complement_R P = (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (3, \\infty) \\]\n\nNow, let's compare the sets:\n- \\( Q = \\{ x \\mid x > 1 \\} = (1, \\infty) \\)\n- \\( \\complement_R Q = (-\\infty, 1] \\)\n\nWe will check each statement:\n\nA: \\( Q \\subseteq P \\)\n- \\( Q = (1, \\infty) \\) and \\( P = [-1, 3] \\). Clearly, \\( Q \\) is not a subset of \\( P \\) because all elements of \\( Q \\) are not in \\( P \\). Therefore, statement A is false.\n\nB: \\( P \\subseteq Q \\)\n- \\( P = [-1, 3] \\) and \\( Q = (1, \\infty) \\). Clearly, \\( P \\) is not a subset of \\( Q \\) because the interval \\([-1, 3]\\) does not include all elements of \\( Q \\). Therefore, statement B is false.\n\nC: \\( (\\complement_R P) \\subseteq Q \\)\n- \\( \\complement_R P = (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (3, \\infty) \\) and \\( Q = (1, \\infty) \\). The intersection of \\( \\complement_R P \\) and \\( Q \\) is \\( (3, \\infty) \\), which is a subset of \\( Q \\). Therefore, statement C is true.\n\nD: \\( P \\subseteq (\\complement_R Q) \\)\n- \\( P = [-1, 3] \\) and \\( \\complement_R Q = (-\\infty, 1] \\). The intersection of \\( P \\) and \\( \\complement_R Q \\) is \\( [-1, 1] \\), which is not equal to \\( P \\). Therefore, statement D is false.\n\nThe correct statement is:\n\\[ \\boxed{C} \\]\n\nHow long is the shortest polygonal path that has an endpoint at the origin and traces out all integer-coordinate points at least once?\n\nTo determine the shortest polygonal path that has an endpoint at the origin and traces out all integer-coordinate points at least once, we need to consider the nature of such a path. The path must visit every integer-coordinate point exactly once and return to the origin. One such path is known as the \"traveling salesman problem for integer-coordinate points\" and can be visualized as a tour that visits each point exactly once and then returns to the starting point.\n\nThe shortest polygonal path that visits all integer-coordinate points and returns to the origin is a path that forms a \"knight's tour\" on the integer lattice. St