nize that the power of point \\(M\\) with respect to both circles is equal. The power of a point with respect to a circle is given by \\(MA \\cdot MB = MC^2\\). Since \\(M\\) is on the radical axis, the power of \\(M\\) is the same along the line \\(MC\\). This implies that the segments \\(MA\\) and \\(MB\\) are equal in length.\n\nNow, consider the triangle \\(AMC\\). Since \\(AM = MC\\) and \\(\\angle AMC = 120^\\circ\\) (because \\(\\angle BAC = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(MC\\) is the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)), we can use the fact that \\(\\angle AMB = 120^\\circ\\).\n\nTo see why, note that \\(\\angle AMB\\) is the angle subtended by the arc \\(AB\\) at the point \\(M\\). Since \\(M\\) is on the radical axis, \\(\\angle AMB\\) is the external angle to \\(\\triangle AMC\\) when extended, and the measure of this external angle is \\(120^\\circ\\).\n\nThus, the measure of \\(\\angle AMB\\) is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).\n\nCalculate:  \n(1) $\\lg 5^2 + \\left(e^{\\frac{1}{2}} - \\pi\\right)^{0} - \\log_2 1$;  \n(2) $\\frac{1}{2}\\log_{10} 25 + \\log_{10} 2 - \\log_{10} \\frac{1}{",
