      "text": "Given the relationships between $x$, $y$, and $z$, we can write the following equations:\n1. The value of $x$ is one-half the value of $y$, which translates to $x = \\frac{1}{2}y$.\n2. The value of $y$ is one-fifth the value of $z$, which can be written as $y = \\frac{1}{5}z$.\n3. We are given that $z = 60$.\n\nTo find the value of $x$, we first need to find the value of $y$ using the equation $y = \\frac{1}{5}z$. Substituting $z = 60$ into this equation, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\ny &= \\frac{1}{5} \\times 60 \\\\\ny &= 12.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNow that we have the value of $y$, we can use the equation $x = \\frac{1}{2}y$ to find $x$. Substituting $y = 12$ into this equation, we get:\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\\\\nx &= 6.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{6}$.",
